\alias{BaseRLagLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Lag Plot Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{lag.plot()} as the grid of scatters it draws: one panel per series
and lag, the series plotted against a shifted copy of itself.

\strong{A grid, not a layer.} Like \code{pairs()}, this call lays out its own panels
and hands nothing to the device's layout calls, so the grid has to come
from the reading. It answers \code{multi_panel = TRUE} and places each layer at
its own cell.

\strong{What a panel pairs.} Measured by tracing \code{graphics::plot.xy} through a
real call -- the panel is \code{plot(lag(X, k), X)}, and \code{lag()} shifts the time
base \emph{back}, so the pair at time \code{t} is

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{x = X[t + k]      the later reading, across
y = X[t]          the earlier one, up
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

over every \code{t} where both indices land inside the series. A negative lag
works out of the same expression and was measured too: \code{set.lags = -1}
gives \verb{x = X[1..n-1]} against \verb{y = X[2..n]}, and \code{set.lags = 0} gives the
series against itself.

\strong{The panels are numbered in draw order.} The nested loop runs series
outermost and lag innermost, and \code{par(mfrow)} fills row by row -- measured
against a \code{grid.echo()} export, a two-column matrix at \code{lags = 2} writes
\code{graphics-plot-1} through \code{graphics-plot-4} for \verb{(a,1) (a,2) (b,1) (b,2)}.
So panel \code{k} sits at row \code{(k - 1) \%/\% ncols + 1}, column
\code{(k - 1) \%\% ncols + 1}.

\strong{A panel's marks are symbols or labels, and \code{labels} decides which.}
\code{lag.plot()} writes the time index at each pair rather than a symbol when
\code{labels} is true, and \code{labels} defaults to \code{do.lines}, which defaults to
\code{n <= 150} -- so the \emph{default} chart is the labelled one. Measured, the
four combinations give:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{labels  do.lines   grobs in a panel
FALSE   FALSE      points
FALSE   TRUE       points, lines
TRUE    FALSE      text
TRUE    TRUE       text, brokenline
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

So \code{do.lines} only adds the joining line and \code{labels} alone decides the
mark, which is why the two are read separately rather than through the
default that ties them together.

Both marks can be outlined, and the export is what says so. Measured on a
real \code{save_html()} of a labelled call, the text grob comes out as one
group per pair, in data order:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{<g id="graphics-plot-1-text-1.1">
  <g id="graphics-plot-1-text-1.1.1" transform="translate(254.32, …)">
  <g id="graphics-plot-1-text-1.1.2" transform="translate(221.70, …)">
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

which is the same shape a \code{points} grob has, with \code{use} elements replaced
by nested \code{g}s. So the selector differs only in the grob name and the
child it reaches for.
}
